<template>
    <section>
        <h2>Outermost Code</h2>
        <p>
            Note that this code section has no name,
            and recall the lp5 code generation algorithm:
            it first gathers, in order, all the unnamed
            code sections,
            then starts recursively expanding the code
            references they contain.
            But because HTML is a (nearly) rigorously nested syntax,
            that means that when you're using lp5 to write
            an HTML5 program (i.e., a single HTML file),
            there will be one and only one unnamed code section
            (unless you want to give <code>doctype</code>
            its very own lonely little code section).
        </p>
        <p>
            Note also that the code here (and this paragraph!)
            contain the dreaded <code>&lt;/script></code>&#8212;the
            one character sequence that must not appear inside of
            a <code>&lt;script></code> element,
            and lp5 keeps code inside a <code>&lt;script></code> element
            when it's not being displayed.
            In both situations (as you can see if you edit this article),
            the solution is to write <code>&amp;lt;/script></code>.
        </p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <code><![CDATA[
            <!doctype html>
            <html lang="en">
                <head>
                    <meta charset="utf-8" >
                    <title>lp5</title>
                </head>
                <body>
                    <script>
                    </@<span>script@</span>>
                </body>
            </html>
        ]]></code>
    </section>
</template>
